=== channel rings: who is handed the leftovers ===
[test] the ring keeps what its previous writer left
  ok   the writer published a byte
  ok   the writer published a byte
  ok   the writer published a byte
  ok   the writer published a byte
  ok   the writer published a byte
  ok   a reader that does not seek is fed the whole ring (control)
  ok   seek_to_latest() reports the five messages it skipped
  ok   a reader that seeks is not fed the leftovers
  ok   the writer published one more byte
  ok   the same reader still sees the next message
[test] a ring outlives the writer that filled it, while anyone holds it
  ok   a writer filled a channel that somebody else holds
  ok   the writer is gone and the message is still there for a later reader
  ok   so a reader joining now does have something to skip
  ok   a writer filled a channel nobody else holds
  ok   with no holder left the section is gone, so there is nothing to be handed
  ok   and the channel built in its place counts no messages
[test] rclcpp::Service and rclcpp::Client skip the ring as they are built
  ok   rclcpp::Service's constructor skips what the request ring already holds
  FAIL rclcpp::Client's constructor skips the leftover answer
  ok   a request sent after the constructors is answered
  ok   and it is the only request this server was handed
  FAIL with this server's answer
[test] a second server of the same name is not handed the first one's requests
  ok   the first server answered its own request
  ok   the first server served exactly one request
  ok   a request published while nothing polls is not served late
  FAIL the second server was handed neither of the previous requests
  FAIL the second server still answers a request of its own
  FAIL and it served exactly that one
[test] a second client of the same name does not read the first one's answer
  ok   the first client got its own answer
  ok   the first server served exactly once
  ok   the second client got an answer
  FAIL and it is this server's answer, not the previous client's
  FAIL the second server served exactly once
[test] a request published before the server existed is dropped, not delivered
  FAIL the server was not handed the request that predates it
  FAIL and that caller is still waiting rather than holding a late answer
  ok   a request sent after the server is up is answered
  FAIL and the server served exactly that one
  ok   with this server's answer
[test] a fresh client's requests are matched to their own answers
  ok   all three requests were answered
  ok   the server was handed three requests, not two or four
  ok   the three payloads arrived in the order they were sent
[test] 50 consecutive restarts of one service name
  FAIL no round of 50 started with a request from an earlier round
  FAIL every round was served exactly once, with its own answer
  FAIL restarting a service name 50 times is clean (stale 49, unanswered 0, wrong 49)

44 checks, 13 failure(s)
